                            <p class="heading__desc" style="color: black;align-items:justify;">
                                <b style="color: #213360;font-size: 25px;">Introduction</b><br>Keloids are a type of
                                scar that forms when the skin heals after an injury or surgery. Unlike regular scars,
                                keloid scars extend beyond the original injury site and may continue to grow over time.
                                While they aren’t harmful to your health, they can affect your appearance and cause
                                discomfort. At Skincity India, we specialize in offering advanced keloid treatments that
                                help reduce the size and visibility of keloid scars. In this blog, we’ll explore the
                                causes of keloids, how they develop, and the available treatments for managing and
                                preventing them.
                                <br>




                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">What Are Keloid Scars?


                                </b><br>
                                A keloid is an overgrowth of scar tissue that forms at the site of a healed skin injury.
                                Unlike a typical scar that stays within the boundaries of the wound, a keloid can grow
                                larger than the original injury and extend into surrounding areas. These scars are
                                raised, thick, and often appear shiny, making them more noticeable.<br>
                                <b style=" font-size: 18px;color: #213360;"> Common Areas for Keloids:</b><br>

                                Ears (often due to piercings)<br>

                                Chest<br>

                                Shoulders<br>

                                Back<br>

                                Jawline<br>









                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">Causes of Keloid Scarring


                                </b><br>
                                Keloid formation occurs when the body produces too much collagen during the healing
                                process. This excessive collagen leads to the formation of a raised, thick scar.
                                Although genetics play a significant role in keloid formation, other factors can
                                contribute, including:<br>
                                <b style=" font-size: 17px;color: #213360;">Genetics:</b> If you have a family history
                                of keloids, you are more likely to develop them.<br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 17px;color: #213360;">Skin type:</b> People with darker skin are
                                more prone to keloids.<br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 17px;color: #213360;">Injury or surgery:</b> Any injury or surgery
                                that involves cutting through the skin, such as piercings, burns, acne, or vaccination
                                sites, can lead to keloid formation.<br>

                                <b style=" font-size: 17px;color: #213360;">Age and hormones: </b> Keloids are more
                                common in people aged 10-30 years and may be influenced by hormonal changes.<br>

                                <br>
                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">Symptoms of Keloids

                                </b><br>
                                Keloid scars typically appear as raised, firm, and thick areas of skin. They may be red,
                                dark brown, or flesh-colored and can grow over time. Although they are not usually
                                painful, keloids can cause discomfort, itching, or tenderness, especially when pressure
                                is applied to the affected area.</b><br>
                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">Keloid Prevention Tips
                                </b><br>
                                While it's difficult to completely prevent keloids, you can minimize your risk by
                                following these steps:<br>

                                Avoid unnecessary skin injuries, especially in areas prone to scarring.<br>

                                Use sunscreen on scarred areas to prevent pigmentation changes and further
                                irritation.<br>

                                Follow medical advice for wound care after surgeries, piercings, or injuries.<br>

                                Consult with a dermatologist for early treatment of any scars that seem to be growing
                                out of control.<br>



                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;"> Why Choose Skincity for Keloid Scar
                                    Treatment?</b><br>

                                At Skincity India, we offer personalized treatments for keloid scars, using the latest
                                technology and expert care to ensure optimal results. Our team of dermatologists works
                                closely with each patient to create a tailored treatment plan for keloid prevention and
                                scar reduction.

                                If you're struggling with keloid scars, book a consultation with Skincity India to
                                explore the best treatment options available for your skin.<br>



                                <b style=" font-size: 25px;color: #213360;">Conclusion</b><br>

                                Keloid scars are a challenging cosmetic concern that can be treated effectively with the
                                right approach. At Skincity India, we specialize in keloid scar treatment using advanced
                                techniques like laser therapy, steroid injections, and surgical options. If you're
                                looking to reduce or manage keloid scars, don’t hesitate to reach out to us for a
                                consultation. Our team will provide personalized care to help you achieve the best
                                possible results.<br>














                               


